THOUGHT: d1 (and possibly d2) can be null because Map.get returns @Nullable. To satisfy NullAway without changing behavior, I should handle nulls explicitly in compare: treat null demographics as size 0 (or some consistent default) and avoid dereferencing them directly. I’ll update the compare method accordingly using sed, then run the build to ensure the NullAway error count decreases by one and no new errors are introduced.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/wala-util && sed -i '684,688c\      @Override
      public int compare(Field o1, Field o2) {
        Demographics d1 = roots.get(o1);
        Demographics d2 = roots.get(o2);
        int s1 = (d1 == null) ? 0 : d1.getTotalSize();
        int s2 = (d2 == null) ? 0 : d2.getTotalSize();
        return s2 - s1;
      }' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/heapTrace/HeapTracer.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1
```

